### Anemic vs Rich Domain Model

An anemic model is a set of classes (or plain objects) that hold data with public getters/setters but almost no behavior — all the actual business logic lives in separate "service" classes that operate on that data from the outside. A rich model puts the behavior on the objects themselves: an `Order` doesn't just expose a public `items` array for services to manipulate, it exposes methods like `addItem()` that enforce invariants internally.

The anemic style looks appealing at first because it maps cleanly onto database tables and feels easy to reason about procedurally, but it quietly defeats the purpose of object-oriented domain modeling: nothing stops two different services from mutating the same object in conflicting, invariant-breaking ways, because the object never protected its own consistency in the first place.

```typescript
// Anemic — data + external service does the work
class Order {
  id: string;
  items: OrderItem[] = [];
  status: string;
  total: number;
}

class OrderService {
  addItem(order: Order, item: OrderItem) {
    order.items.push(item);                 // nothing stops this even if order is "shipped"
    order.total = order.items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price, 0);
  }
}

// Rich — behavior lives with the data, invariants are structurally protected
class Order {
  private items: OrderItem[] = [];
  private status: OrderStatus = OrderStatus.Draft;

  addItem(item: OrderItem): void {
    if (this.status !== OrderStatus.Draft) {
      throw new InvalidOrderStateError("cannot modify a submitted order");
    }
    this.items.push(item);
  }

  get total(): Money {
    return this.items.reduce((sum, i) => sum.add(i.price), Money.zero());
  }
}
```

Enforcing a rich model at the project level takes more than writing classes with methods — you generally need to make the anemic shortcut structurally hard to reach: keep fields `private`, expose only intention-revealing methods, and if you use an ORM, use a mapping layer (or something like TypeORM's embeddables, or a manual `toPersistence`/`fromPersistence` pair) so persistence concerns never force you to expose public setters just to satisfy the ORM's needs.

The trade-off: a rich model costs more upfront design effort (you have to actually think about what operations and invariants an object should expose, instead of just mirroring a table), and it can feel like friction when a junior developer just wants to "quickly patch a field." But it's what actually prevents invalid states from ever existing, instead of relying on every caller remembering to run validation logic correctly and consistently.

**Common pitfall:** starting with a rich model, then quietly adding a public setter "temporarily" to unblock one feature (e.g. `set status(s: OrderStatus) { this.status = s; }`), which reintroduces exactly the anemic bypass the design was meant to prevent — invariants stop being guaranteed the moment any field becomes publicly writable.

