### Structuring an Order Bounded Context

At senior level, the interesting part isn't just "what's an aggregate" — it's how you lay out the module so the boundaries actually enforce the rules instead of just being a folder convention. A typical structure separates the domain layer (pure business logic, no framework or I/O) from application (use-case orchestration) and infrastructure (persistence, messaging):

```
order-context/
  domain/
    Order.ts            // aggregate root
    OrderLine.ts         // entity within the aggregate
    Money.ts             // value object
    OrderPlaced.ts       // domain event
    OrderRepository.ts   // interface only — no implementation here
  application/
    PlaceOrderUseCase.ts // orchestrates: load, mutate, save, publish
  infrastructure/
    PostgresOrderRepository.ts // implements domain/OrderRepository.ts
    InMemoryEventBus.ts
```

The reasoning behind this split: the `domain/` folder should be importable and testable with zero I/O and zero framework dependencies — if you can `import` a database driver inside `Order.ts`, the boundary has already failed. That's what actually lets you unit test business rules in milliseconds without spinning up Postgres.

```typescript
// domain/Order.ts
export class Order {
  private lines: OrderLine[] = [];
  private status: OrderStatus = OrderStatus.Draft;
  private domainEvents: DomainEvent[] = [];

  constructor(public readonly id: OrderId, public readonly customerId: CustomerId) {}

  addLine(product: ProductRef, quantity: number, unitPrice: Money): void {
    if (this.status !== OrderStatus.Draft) {
      throw new InvalidOrderStateError(this.status);
    }
    this.lines.push(new OrderLine(product, quantity, unitPrice));
  }

  place(): void {
    if (this.lines.length === 0) throw new EmptyOrderError();
    this.status = OrderStatus.Placed;
    this.domainEvents.push(new OrderPlaced(this.id, this.total()));
  }

  total(): Money {
    return this.lines.reduce((sum, l) => sum.add(l.subtotal()), Money.zero("USD"));
  }

  pullEvents(): DomainEvent[] {
    const events = this.domainEvents;
    this.domainEvents = [];
    return events;
  }
}

// application/PlaceOrderUseCase.ts — orchestration, no business rules of its own
export class PlaceOrderUseCase {
  constructor(private repo: OrderRepository, private bus: EventBus) {}

  async execute(orderId: OrderId): Promise<void> {
    const order = await this.repo.findById(orderId);
    order.place();                 // business rule lives in the aggregate
    await this.repo.save(order);
    for (const event of order.pullEvents()) await this.bus.publish(event);
  }
}
```

Note `Money` is a Value Object created with an explicit currency (`Money.zero("USD")`) rather than a bare number, specifically so arithmetic across mismatched currencies fails loudly instead of silently producing a wrong total — this is the kind of invariant a rich Value Object buys you that a plain `number` field can't.

**Common pitfall:** letting the `application/` layer reach past the aggregate and manipulate `order`'s internal fields directly to "save a method call" — for example doing `order['status'] = 'placed'` via a cast. That silently breaks the entire point of putting invariants in the aggregate, and it's much easier to introduce accidentally in TypeScript than people expect, since `as any` casts compile fine.

